<p>
  Notice what the right side <em>doesn't have</em>: loading flags, fallback branches,
  effect dependencies, race-condition guards, "is it undefined because it's loading or
  because it's empty" ambiguity (<a href="10-suspense-streaming.html#orthogonal">§10.6</a
  >). That code was never simplified; it was never written. Multiply that by every
  data-bearing component on every page, and "simplicity" stops being an abstract virtue
  and becomes the difference between a codebase you can reason about and one you can't.
  <Chip k="react" />
  <Chip k="very" />
</p>

<h2 id="perceived">4.3 Perceived performance, and where streaming fits</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Perceived performance</strong> is how fast a page <em>feels</em>, which is not
  the same thing as how long it takes to finish. Users don't experience totals; they
  experience milestones: when something first appears, when the content they came for is
  readable, whether things move around while they wait, and when the page responds to
  input. A page that paints meaningful content in 300ms and quietly finishes a slow widget
  at 3s <em>feels fast</em>. A page that shows a spinner until everything is ready at 1.5s
  feels slower, despite technically "finishing" sooner.
</p>

<Callout kind="info" title="The Next.js docs, on why this matters">
  <p>
    "SSR with React and Next.js helps improve the perceived loading performance by showing
    a non-interactive page to the user as soon as possible." (<a
      href="https://nextjs.org/docs/14/app/building-your-application/rendering/server-components"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      >Server Components, Next.js docs</a
    >)
  </p>
</Callout>

<p>
  Read that carefully: <em>non-interactive</em>, <em>as soon as possible</em>. The docs
  are conceding something and claiming something. The concession: the first paint isn't
  interactive yet: hydration hasn't happened. The claim: that trade is worth it, because
  seeing real content immediately dominates the user's perception of speed. People spend
  the first moments on a page <em>reading</em>, not clicking; SSR uses that window to be
  useful while the JavaScript catches up.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Streaming is the same principle applied within a single response.</strong> Plain
  SSR has a weakness: if the server waits for <em>all</em> data before sending anything,
  the slowest query gates the entire page. Streaming removes that gate: the server sends
  the known immediately (structure, containers, everything already resolved) and flushes
  the slow parts into their reserved slots as they finish, over the same response (<a
    href="10-suspense-streaming.html#streaming"
    >§10.2</a
  >). Perceived performance is governed by when the first meaningful content lands and
  whether the page holds still afterward; streaming makes the first independent of your
  slowest query, and containers (<a href="09-containers-content-shifting.html">§9</a>)
  guarantee the second. That is the whole Part B playbook in one sentence, powered by the
  fact that rendering happens on a server that can flush incrementally.
</p>

<h2 id="page-cache">
  4.4 The cached page: maximized server content becomes a cacheable artifact
</h2>
<p>
  There's a compounding payoff hiding in all of this. Once a page is rendered on the
  server, its rendered output, the HTML plus the RSC payload that describes it (<a
    href="#routing"
    >§4.5</a
  >), is a concrete <em>artifact</em>. And artifacts can be cached.
</p>
<p>
  Next.js does exactly that. Routes that don't depend on request-time information are
  rendered <strong>once</strong> (at build time, or on first request, or on a revalidation
  schedule), and the cached output is served to every subsequent visitor without
  re-rendering: effectively a static site, per route. When part of a page
  <em>does</em> need request-time data, the modern model (Partial Prerendering /
  <code>cacheComponents</code>) caches the static shell and streams only the dynamic holes
  into it. The mechanics (<code>"use cache"</code>, revalidation, cache tags) live in
  <a href="13-dedupe-caching.html">§13</a>; what belongs here is the architectural point:
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Only server-rendered content can participate in this cache.</strong>
  <Chip k="next" />
  <Chip k="critical" /> The framework can only cache output it
  produces; content fetched from the browser after hydration is invisible to it: every
  user re-pays the full cost of every client-side request, forever. So each piece of
  content you move to the server is upgraded twice: it arrives in the first paint
  <em>and</em> it becomes eligible to be rendered once and served to everyone. Maximizing
  the known isn't just making one request faster; it's making the page progressively
  cheaper to serve at all. This is also the trap the audit found us in: a client-rendered
  tree opts out of the entire caching layer silently, page by page, with no error to tell
  you what you gave up.
</p>

<h2 id="routing">4.5 Routing: server routing, client routing, and the RSC payload</h2>
<p>
  One reasonable worry remains: if the server renders everything, doesn't every click
  become a full page reload: white flash, re-downloaded assets, lost state? This is where
  the App Router is cleverer than classic server rendering, and understanding it requires
  one piece of vocabulary: the <strong>RSC payload</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  The RSC payload is a compact, streamable serialization of a rendered server component
  tree. It contains the rendered output of your server components, placeholders marking
  where client components go (plus references to their JS chunks), and any props passed
  from server to client. It is what the server <em>actually produces</em> when it renders;
  HTML is generated from it for first visits. Think of it as "the rendered page, described
  as data", which is precisely what makes client-side merging possible.
</p>
<p>Routing then works in two modes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>First visit: server routing.</strong> The browser requests a URL; the server
    renders the full tree and responds with a complete HTML document (with the RSC payload
    embedded for hydration). Real content paints before JavaScript loads: everything
    §4.1–§4.3 promised.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Every navigation after: client routing.</strong> Clicking a
    <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> does <em>not</em> reload the document. The router requests
    the RSC payload for just the route segments that change, and React merges it into the
    page in place. Shared layouts don't re-render and their state survives (<a
      href="03-mental-model.html#rendering-model"
      >§3.1</a
    >); only the content that differs is replaced. And because
    <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> prefetches payloads for links in the viewport, the merge
    often happens from cache - navigation lands near-instantly.
  </li>
</ul>

