<p>
  This is the answer to the worry, and the reason SSR here doesn't mean "a website from
  2005." You get server rendering's first paint <em>and</em> an SPA's navigation feel,
  from the same architecture, without building either by hand. But notice it's
  conditional: the seamless merge works because the server renders segments the router can
  splice. A page that client-fetches everything after mount gets neither benefit: its
  first visit is a shell, and its navigations re-run the whole fetch choreography (<a
    href="11-blocking-requests.html#client"
    >§11.4</a
  >).
</p>

<h3 id="prefetching">Links and prefetching</h3>
<p>
  The instant-navigation story above hangs on one component: <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code>.
  Internal navigation always goes through <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code>, never a plain
  <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>: an anchor tag opts out of the entire model with a full document
  load: layouts torn down, state cleared, scroll reset.
  <Chip k="next" />
  <Chip k="critical" /> But <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> does more
  than route on the client: it <strong>prefetches</strong>. When a link enters the
  viewport (or is hovered), Next.js quietly loads the target route in the background, so
  by the time the user actually clicks, the payload the router needs is usually already
  sitting on the client, and the navigation resolves without waiting on the network at
  all.
</p>

<pre
            class="code" is:raw
