&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Link</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/programs"</span>&gt;Programs&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Link</span>&gt;

<span class="tok-com">{/* A plain anchor opts out of everything: full document load, no prefetch. */}</span>
&lt;a <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/contact"</span>&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;

<span class="tok-com">{/* Prefetching can be disabled per link: the right call for large lists of
   links (an infinite-scroll table of rows), where eagerly prefetching every
   visible row is wasted work. Prefetch-on-hover is the middle ground. */}</span>
&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Link</span> <span class="tok-prop">prefetch</span>={<span class="tok-kw">false</span>} <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/programs/42"</span>&gt;Program 42&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Link</span>&gt;</pre>

<p>
  How much can be prefetched is, once again, a known/unknown question, this time asked
  about a route the user hasn't visited yet:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Static routes are prefetched in full.</strong> The route was prerendered (<a
      href="#page-cache"
      >§4.4</a
    >), so its complete payload already exists as a cached artifact; the client can fetch
    all of it ahead of the click and navigate entirely from cache.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Dynamic routes are prefetched partially, or not at all.</strong> The server
    can't render request-time content before the request exists. But if the route has a
    <code>loading.tsx</code>, Next.js prefetches everything that <em>is</em> knowable
    ahead of time: the shared layouts and the loading skeleton. The known travels early;
    only the unknown waits for the click.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3 id="dynamic-loading">
  The slow-transition trap: dynamic routes without <code>loading.tsx</code>
</h3>
<p>
  Follow that second bullet to its failure mode, because the
  <a
    href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/linking-and-navigating#dynamic-routes-without-loadingtsx"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener"
    >docs call it out</a
  >
  as the number-one thing that makes transitions feel slow. A dynamic route with no
  <code>loading.tsx</code> has nothing prefetchable and nothing to show on click, so when
  the user navigates, <em>nothing happens</em> until the server finishes rendering. Click…
  wait… still the old page. In the docs' words, it "can give the users the impression that
  the app is not responding." Every dynamic route should have a
  <code>loading.tsx</code> (or an equivalent Suspense boundary).
  <Chip k="next" />
  <Chip k="very" />
</p>
<p>
  With the file present, the whole sequence inverts. The layouts and skeleton were
  partially prefetched, so the navigation <strong>commits immediately</strong>: the URL
  changes, the known structure paints on click, and the server's content streams into the
  reserved fallback when it arrives (<a href="10-suspense-streaming.html#streaming"
    >§10.2</a
  >). The docs enumerate the benefits: immediate visual feedback, shared layouts that stay
  interactive while the route loads (the user can even interrupt the navigation and click
  elsewhere), better TTFB/FCP/TTI. But notice what the pattern <em>is</em>:
  contain-the-unknown, applied to navigation. The known part of the next page travels
  ahead of the click; the unknown part streams in behind it, into space that was reserved
  for it.
</p>
<p>
  Three edge notes close the loop. If a dynamic segment's pages are enumerable ahead of
  time, <code>generateStaticParams</code> promotes them to prerendered static routes: full
  prefetch, no fallback needed at all. On slow networks the prefetch may not finish before
  the click; <code>useLinkStatus</code> exposes a <code>pending</code> flag for an
  immediate, debounced hint on the link itself. And prefetching can't start until
  <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> hydrates (it is a client component), which is one more reason
  to keep first-render JavaScript small (<a href="06-first-render-js.html">§6</a>).
</p>

